text:
Index of texts to illustrated manuscripts
One row per CTS URN and manuscript (&gt; 10,000 rows) is written to the delimited-text file textindex.cex in the form mss|text|location. .

Links (textindex.py). Two paged queries run, at nesting depth 1 (M has_part C) and depth 2 (M has_part C1, C1 has_part C). Each returns, for every component that carries a text: manuscript ark, the component’s minmax_folios, and the ark of the text’s Work (C related_text T, T work W). Components without a Work-linked text are not seen.
Filter. A link is kept only if the manuscript ark is in mss.cex and the Work ark is in texts.cex. That guarantees both keys exist in the other files. text is the CTS URN from texts.cex.
Location of text in the manuscript is taken from minmax_folios, tidied but never reinterpreted:

trailing * removed (its meaning in the source is unknown);
– and -- become -; a f./ff. prefix is dropped (p. is kept);
leading zeros dropped (082-146 → 82-146);
32r-v is written out as 32r-32v;
folio labels stay strings (63bisr); a bare number stays bare (168-170), because the source does not say which side of the leaf is meant;
lists and free text (12-21v, 104-133v) are only tidied. An empty location means Biblissima records none (about 14% of rows).

De-duplication. Rows are unique triples. If a manuscript has the same text once with a location and once without, only the located row is kept. Output is sorted.
A summary prints the counts and the most common location patterns (digits shown as 9, letters as a) so odd formats are easy to spot.
